{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "single_phase",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "At fixed Delta, conductivity is controlled by one smooth disorder-softened Arrhenius denominator whose temperature dependence follows a single shared crossover shape, so log(sigma) versus 1/T has one universal bending pattern after rescaling by k_B*T/Delta.",
      "child_relation": "Retain thermally assisted hopping and disorder softening, but relax the assumption that the softening strength is independent of barrier scale except through k_B*T/Delta; allow the efficiency of disorder-induced barrier reduction itself to weaken for sufficiently large Delta, producing barrier-dependent curvature in the apparent activation law.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "T",
        "Delta"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "Across low-Delta and high-Delta subsets, the temperature-induced softening of the apparent barrier bends log(sigma) differently: high-Delta samples retain closer-to-Arrhenius behavior deeper into the same k_B*T/Delta range, while low-Delta samples show earlier curvature/saturation. One shared parent parameter set cannot make the crossover curvature depend separately on Delta beyond the existing ratio structure."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "theta",
        "expression": "k_B*T/Delta",
        "role": "thermal-to-barrier ratio controlling activation of disorder-softening"
      },
      {
        "name": "soft_frac",
        "expression": "Delta/(Delta + k_B*T)",
        "role": "fraction measuring how strongly the nominal barrier still dominates over thermal energy"
      },
      {
        "name": "barrier_vs_disorder",
        "expression": "Delta/(k_B*T_dis)",
        "role": "dimensionless barrier scale relative to the disorder-renormalization temperature scale, useful for detecting barrier-dependent softening efficiency"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when Delta spans a range comparable to and larger than k_B*T_dis and temperatures also reach intermediate theta = k_B*T/Delta ~ 0.1 to 1, where disorder softening is active but not fully saturated.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "Within training, different Delta slices can already show unequal curvature of log(sigma) versus 1/T at similar theta; in holdout, the same mechanism extends this contrast to larger Delta where the softening suppression becomes stronger.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent when the added barrier-scale dependence vanishes, e.g. in the limit of zero strength for the new dependence or when Delta is small compared with its new crossover scale so the extra factor is effectively unity.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The current law assumes a single universal temperature-crossover shape for disorder softening once expressed through k_B*T/Delta, with Delta affecting the response mainly through the Arrhenius numerator and the existing ratio factors; it omits the realistic possibility that very deep barriers are less efficiently softened by disorder-mediated pathway selection even at the same thermal fraction.",
    "baseline_assumption": "A single effective activation scale represents the disorder landscape, with disorder softening governed by one universal crossover profile that does not have an additional explicit dependence on the absolute barrier scale.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Barrier-selective percolative filtering can become less effective for deeper localized states because accessible alternative paths remain energetically too costly, so the disorder-induced reduction of apparent activation energy depends on absolute Delta as well as on k_B*T/Delta.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "T",
      "Delta",
      "T_dis"
    ],
    "rationale": "Parent response relationship: at fixed sigma0, k_B, T_dis, beta_shape, and gamma_cross, increasing T always increases sigma by lowering the effective Arrhenius denominator through one common softening law, while increasing Delta decreases sigma. The scientific gap is that the parent encodes only one phase of disorder-softening curvature across all barriers: once plotted against theta = k_B*T/Delta, every sample shares the same crossover shape apart from overall scaling by Delta. A realistic refinement is to relax that universal-shape assumption and let the disorder-softening efficiency itself depend on the absolute barrier scale. Varied conditions: T and Delta are independently sampled; carrier density, geometry, disorder statistics, and weak-field conditions remain fixed. Concrete contrast: for two samples measured over similar theta in the admissible range, one with Delta << k_B*T_dis and one with Delta >= k_B*T_dis, the child can show stronger bending away from simple Arrhenius behavior for the lower-Delta sample and delayed/smaller bending for the higher-Delta sample. The parent with one shared parameter set cannot reproduce such barrier-dependent curvature beyond its fixed rational dependence on theta. This changes the scored target mapping sigma(T,Delta), not merely an auxiliary interpretation. Limitation: this is most defensible when the experiment actually spans a broad enough Delta range; if all Delta are narrowly clustered, the added dependence may be hard to identify."
  },
